Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1 (title): Texting is "impossible" for people with ADHD
→ Unsupported/false claim — this is a significant overstatement that contradicts the ICD-11 framework. ADHD symptoms (distractibility, forgetfulness) create difficulty and inconsistency with tasks like texting, not impossibility — ICD-11's "boundary with normality" section frames ADHD as a difference in degree and persistence of universal human struggles, not an absolute inability to perform a task. The video's own body content describes someone "getting sidetracked" and forgetting to reply — a description of inconsistent difficulty, not impossibility — which directly contradicts and is materially misrepresented by its own title

Claim 2: A person with ADHD may get sidetracked while typing a reply to a message, put the phone down to focus on something else, and then forget they were supposed to reply
→ Supported — matches ICD-11 inattention criteria: "being easily distracted by extraneous stimuli or thoughts not related to the task at hand" and "being forgetful in daily activities; having difficulty remembering to complete upcoming daily tasks or activities"

Claim 3: This forgetfulness ("amnesia") is never malicious/intentional
→ Supported — consistent with ADHD's classification in ICD-11 as a neurodevelopmental disorder involving genuine attentional impairment rather than intentional behavior; forgetfulness and distractibility are described as core symptom features, not deliberate choices

Claim 4: This pattern can make others feel ignored or mistreated
→ Supported (reasonable, general) — consistent with ICD-11's additional clinical features noting ADHD "can strain interpersonal relationships across the lifespan," which would plausibly include a partner feeling ignored due to unanswered messages

Claim 5: In romantic relationships, ADHD can add an extra layer of frustration, and each partner in a relationship has particular needs
→ Supported (general) — consistent with ICD-11's note that ADHD strains relationships "including those with family members, peers and romantic partners"; the point about differing partner needs is a reasonable, non-contradicted general relationship observation

